{
  "dom_id": "step-q171159",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=${{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- First, we find the prime factorization of $9.$ We easily deduce that $9={3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $15.$ We easily deduce that $15=3\\times 5.$\n\n- Then, we notice that $2$ is a prime number.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =3\\times 5 \\\\ 2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $9$, $15,$ and $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$",
